.\" AXE modifications: use bare BSD examples and distinguish user selectors.
.TP
.B ps
Default: your processes attached to your terminal. Useful for
"what's still running in this shell session?".
.TP
.B ps \-e
Every process on the system, default columns.
.TP
.B ps \-ef
Every process, full format. The most-typed \fBps\fR invocation in
the wild.
.TP
.B ps aux
BSD-style every-process listing with CPU, memory, and state
columns.
.TP
.B ps \-p 1234
Show only PID 1234, in default format.
.TP
.B ps \-p 1234,5678 \-f
Several PIDs in full format.
.TP
.B ps \-U alice
All processes with real user \fIalice\fR.
.TP
.B ps \-U alice,bob \-f
Multiple real users in full format. UIDs as numbers also work
(\fBps \-U 1000,1001\fR).
.TP
.B ps \-u alice
All processes with effective user \fIalice\fR.
.TP
.B ps \-e \-o pid=,comm=
Every process with only PID and command-name columns, without headers.
.TP
.B ps aux | grep firefox
The classic find-this-process pipeline. \fBpgrep\fR(1) is usually a
better fit.
